<Project Sdk="Godot.NET.Sdk/4.4.0">

  <!--
    In SRDCombat.sln, so CI compiles it. This project sat *outside* the solution until
    2026-08-15, on the stated reasoning that including it could break a merge on a
    runner that has .NET 8 and no Godot. That reasoning was wrong, and the plan doc's
    Phase 7 section had already recorded why: `Godot.NET.Sdk` is a NuGet package, so the
    build needs no Godot installed. Checked rather than argued — a cold build with
    `client/obj` and `.godot/mono/temp` deleted resolves GodotSharp.dll from
    `~/.nuget/packages/godotsharp/4.4.0/lib/net8.0/`, never from the Godot on PATH, and
    succeeds on net8.0 with 0 warnings. Two documents disagreeing about a fact is what
    settling it empirically is for.

    The cost of the exclusion was the whole point: CI runs bare `dotnet restore`, `build`
    and `test` from the repository root, which resolve the solution, so 5,065 lines — the
    entire graphical client, every line a player touches — were never compiled by the gate
    that protects the engine. Nothing stopped a `Core` signature change from breaking the
    client silently, and no test covers it either way. See README.md beside this file.

    **The solution maps `Release|Any CPU` to this project's `Debug`, and that is correct
    rather than a slip to tidy.** Godot.NET.Sdk defines `Debug;ExportDebug;ExportRelease`
    and no plain `Release` — confirmed with `dotnet msbuild -getProperty:Configurations` —
    so pointing the solution's Release leg at a `Release` that does not exist here would
    fail the build. Both CI legs therefore compile this project in `Debug`, which costs
    nothing: the point of gating it is catching a compile break, and a break breaks in
    either configuration. The `ExportRelease` configuration is what a shipped build would
    use, and nothing exports the client yet.

    Directory.Build.props still reaches here — MSBuild walks up from the *project's*
    directory, not from wherever the build was launched — so TargetFramework, Nullable,
    ImplicitUsings and TreatWarningsAsErrors all arrive from the repository root and the
    client is held to the engine's standard without restating it. Verified with
    `dotnet msbuild -getProperty:...` rather than assumed; an earlier version of this
    comment claimed the opposite.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <EnableDynamicLoading>true</EnableDynamicLoading>
    <RootNamespace>SRDCombat.Viewer</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\src\SRDCombat.Game\SRDCombat.Game.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
